d the mistake gained it nothing. Thirty-four cases were written out in full twice, by the executor before any runner existed and by an outside reviewer without sight of the executor's, and the statement computed on both. Of 679 numbers in the executor's specifications 38 are fixed in value by the cases' words, 118 in order only and 523 are supplied. On nineteen cases from Pettigrew, Kolodny, Carroll et al. and Noggle the statement gives the source's verdict on the act in 15 under the executor's specifications as committed, in 12 once three numbers the reviewer found verdict-directed take its neutral values, and in 12 under the reviewer's; Pettigrew's unhappy parent and Kolodny's just deterrent go against the source under both. The two specifications give another verdict in kind in 8 of 34, five through her informed rating of the course that changes her. None of the seven cases that passed the earlier statement passes, five being caught only by a test for lowered options or knowledge that the executor added; of twelve acts called acceptable the third line flags five or six, each justified by what someone else is owed or entitled to. The twelve formal items hold as stated or with a stated condition; two baselines within the tolerance can give third-line values a whole scale apart, and the inducing test is wrong on two instances. The reviewer gives three cases, accepted by the executor as plain subversion, in which the adviser decides which of two options she rates the same she takes and every line is zero. No verdict differs between the two settings. No Lean; nothing registered<br>MIXED — the join of the account to the corrigibility post is written in Lean, 117 declarations, kernel-checked, none registered: a gate on whole histories made of three predicates and a third-line value, the post's scoring built from it, and the post's Theorems 1 and 3 at that pair. Of the dispatch's six claims: that the post's theorems apply holds by construction and instantiation, the ranges the post asks for being fields of the gate; that a history on which the adviser gained through a mistake of hers scores below every legitimate violation-free history is proved from those ranges, strictly and with no margin; that two legitimate histories with one rating have one evaluation, and that a history on which her information is not sufficient is not legitimate, hold by definition; the bound on what deferring to her verdict costs is proved on a finite space, and holds on a legitimate history because soundness is there defined as a bound on the same miscalibration, with a tolerance; that the third line is never negative, is zero at the baseline and is at least its older form is proved in full, with an instance on which it is 1/5 and the older form is -1/10. The guarantee on what she attains composes with the deferring bound only under a hypothesis linking what she observes to what the adviser knows; on an instance without it every valuation is calibrated in the deferring sense, she has no regret, and she attains 7/20 against a move worth 1/2. Of four further statements: that worsening her options lowers her evaluation, and that the authority module covers control of her act between options she rates the same, cannot be stated with what exists; the post's feedback hypothesis passes from the feedback she gives to the evaluation the join defines under a stated condition and fails on an instance without it; the gate's value is the segment gate's value form, and its predicate is unrelated to the segment gate's. A gate on which every history is legitimate satisfies every theorem: the predicates are given, and nothing connects them to the conduct the post's guarantees are meant to constrain.<br>MIXED — the scoring theorem of the dispatch is in Lean on a finite space, 70 declarations, kernel-checked, none registered, with each of its dependencies a hypothesis of the theorems that use it: the bound on her expected true utility; the bound on the chance that the adviser wronged her, which uses neither of the two assumptions that her evaluation and her verdict do not run above her true utility; the bound on expected detected violations, which uses neither of those nor detection; and the lemma that a course's expected score is at most its audited evaluation on the histories without a wrong. A witness inhabits the whole package with every tolerance positive, and for each of five dependencies an instance has it alone fail and its bound fail. Of the assumptions about her: that her audited evaluation does not run above what she would say shown everything follows, two-sided, from her coherence, authorship and transparency of the actual process to her model, with her evaluation her credence's expectation of a fixed function and her prior the law of the world, and at each thing the adviser knows only if its record carries what it knows. It does not follow from conduct: an adviser whose honest errors fall both ways leaves her evaluation off by a quarter at each thing it believes, and one that acts on the course it estimates higher leaves it an eighth above that course's quality on average, and neither does wrong. That her verdict as she is does not run above her verdict with her valuing unshaped follows from reason mediation only for a course that leaves the inputs to her valuing as the baseline does. With the two assumptions made only where she was not wronged, the welfare bound holds where the penalty times the detection rate is at least one, so the welfare guarantee rests on detecting the adviser's wrongs. Detection, an honest course and the adviser's near-optimality are not derivable; independence of the audit draws and the false-positive rate come from the audit's design. On twenty cases an outside reviewer wrote from the vocabulary alone, the test of responsibility that reads the adviser's gain is wrong on 8 and the one that reads a declared reference on 14. Nothing registered; every component open or a living note. | lean-proved | 